e United States was a principal antagonist in the Cold War, leading the Western Bloc and advocating for containment of communism."}, {"sourceSlug": "collapse-of-soviet-union", "sourceName": "Collapse of the Soviet Union", "verb": "SUCCEEDS", "targetSlug": "era-cold-war-era", "targetName": "Cold War Era", "context": "The collapse of the Soviet Union in 1991 formally concluded the Cold War Era, dissolving one of its two primary belligerents."}], "places": [{"name": "Berlin, Germany", "role": "Divided City"}, {"name": "Washington, D.C., United States", "role": "Western Bloc Command"}, {"name": "Moscow, Russia", "role": "Eastern Bloc Command"}], "texts": [], "externalLinks": [], "tags": ["timeframe", "era", "cold war era"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "The Cold War Era is a foundational period in the Annals chronological framework, used to classify entities active from 1947 CE to 1991 CE.", "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-05-09T12:25:20Z", "editorId": "ai-enrichment-bot:gemini-2.5-flash", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "", "newValue": "The Cold War Era, a period of intense geopolitical tension and ideological conflict spanning roughly from 1947 to 1991, fundamentally reshaped the global order following World War II. It was characterized by a bipolar standoff between two nuclear-armed superpowers: the United States and its Western allies (primarily NATO members), advocating liberal democracy and capitalism, and the Soviet Union and its Eastern Bloc allies (Warsaw Pact nations), championing communism and state socialism. This era, global in its reach, manifested as proxy wars, an incessant arms race, extensive espionage, and a fierce competition for technological and ideological supremacy, without direct military confrontation between the principal adversaries.

Key events defining this period include the Berlin Blockade (1948-1949), the Korean War (1950-1953), the Space Race inaugurated by Sputnik (1957), the construction of the Berlin Wall (1961), the Cuban Missile Crisis (1962) which brought the world to the brink of nuclear war, and the protracted Vietnam War (1955-1975). Decolonization movements across Africa and Asia often became battlegrounds for superpower influence, as seen in Angola and Afghanistan, while nuclear proliferation became a constant global concern, driving strategic arms limitation talks like SALT I and II.

The legacy of the Cold War is profound, leading to the dissolution of the Soviet Union in 1991 and the emergence of the United States as the sole global superpower. It fostered the development of sophisticated surveillance technologies, shaped international institutions, and left behind a complex tapestry of political alliances and regional conflicts, many of which continue to reverberate today. The ideological contest also significantly influenced cultural production, from spy thrillers to anti-war anthems, leaving an indelible mark on global consciousness."}, {"timestamp": "2026-05-09T12:25:20Z", "editorId": "ai-enrichment-bot:gemini-2.5-flash", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": ["The ideological divergence between capitalist democracies and communist totalitarianism intensified following World War II.", "The division of post-war Germany and Europe into Soviet and Western spheres of influence created immediate geopolitical friction.", "The development of atomic weapons by the United States in 1945 prompted a security dilemma and an arms race with the Soviet Union."], "newValue": ["The ideological divergence between Soviet communism and Western liberal democracy intensified after their uneasy alliance during World War II.", "The post-World War II power vacuum in Europe and Asia created opportunities for both the United States and the Soviet Union to expand their spheres of influence.", "Winston Churchill's 'Iron Curtain' speech in 1946 vividly articulated the growing division of Europe and solidified a sense of impending conflict."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-05-09T12:25:20Z", "editorId": "ai-enrichment-bot:gemini-2.5-flash", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": ["The collapse of the Soviet Union in 1991 marked the end of the bipolar world order and the emergence of new geopolitical alignments.", "The proliferation of nuclear weapons and the establishment of international arms control treaties profoundly shaped global security policy.", "Proxy conflicts 